
<% content_for :title, "Impulsivity Relapse Plan | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="relapse-plan-page relapse-plan-impulsivity">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="relapse-plan-hero">
    <div class="relapse-plan-container">

      <%= link_to "← ALL RELAPSE PLANS",
          toolbox_relapse_plans_path,
          class: "relapse-plan-back" %>

      <p class="relapse-plan-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / RELAPSE PLANS / IMPULSIVITY
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>BEFORE</span>
        <span>"FUCK IT"</span>
        <span>TAKES OVER.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="relapse-plan-intro">
        YOUR PLAN FOR THE SPACE BETWEEN
        THE URGE AND THE ACTION.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 01 / YOUR PATTERN -->
  <section class="relapse-plan-section relapse-plan-pink">
    <div class="relapse-plan-container">

      <p class="relapse-plan-label">
        01 / YOUR PATTERN
      </p>

      <h2>
        HOW FAST<br>
        DOES IT HAPPEN?
      </h2>

      <div class="relapse-plan-copy">

        <p>
          Impulsivity doesn't always announce itself.
          Sometimes you have an idea, and before you've
          really considered it, you're already acting.
          The gap between wanting something and doing
          it becomes so small that the decision barely
          feels like a decision.
        </p>

        <p>
          With bipolar disorder, that gap can shrink
          even further during mania. Your thoughts
          move faster, your confidence increases,
          and everything can feel urgent and
          completely reasonable. You're not
          necessarily thinking about consequences.
          You're thinking about what you want
          right now.
        </p>

        <p>
          Alcohol can enter the picture just as quickly.
          You might be bored, frustrated, restless,
          in pain, or simply looking for a good time.
          Suddenly, drinking sounds appealing.
          Instead of considering whether it's a good
          idea, you start figuring out how to do it.
        </p>

        <p>
          Sometimes it's the familiar "fuck it."
          You've already convinced yourself that
          you deserve the drink, that one time
          won't matter, or that you'll deal
          with the consequences later.
        </p>

        <p>
          The same pattern can show up in spending,
          sending messages, starting projects,
          or making commitments. The impulse
          feels like an instruction, and
          acting on it feels like relief.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>
            The goal isn't to stop having impulses.
            It's to recognize when wanting something
            is turning into doing it without thinking.
          </strong>
        </p>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 02 / THE MOMENT BEFORE -->
  <section class="relapse-plan-section relapse-plan-black">
    <div class="relapse-plan-container">

      <p class="relapse-plan-label">
        02 / THE MOMENT BEFORE
      </p>

      <h2>
        CATCH THE<br>
        SALES PITCH.
      </h2>

      <div class="relapse-plan-grid">

        <!-- TRIGGERS -->
        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>YOUR TRIGGERS</h3>

          <p>
            Pay attention to boredom, restlessness,
            frustration, anger, loneliness,
            physical pain, and the desire
            for something exciting.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sometimes you want relief.
            Sometimes you want pleasure.
            Sometimes an opportunity appears
            unexpectedly, and you start
            making decisions before
            considering whether you
            actually want the consequences.
          </p>

          <p>
            Familiar places and routines
            can make the impulse stronger.
            Walking past alcohol in a store,
            having an unstructured evening,
            or remembering how drinking
            used to feel can start
            the bargaining process.
          </p>

          <p>
            Watch your mood as well.
            Less sleep, racing thoughts,
            increased confidence, unusual
            spending, and a sudden need
            to act on every idea may
            signal mania rather than
            ordinary impulsivity.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>
              When several warning signs
              appear together, don't rely
              on your current confidence
              as proof that you're fine.
            </strong>
          </p>

        </div>


        <!-- EXCUSES -->
        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>YOUR GO-TO EXCUSES</h3>

          <p>
            "Just one won't hurt."
          </p>

          <p>
            "I deserve to have some fun."
          </p>

          <p>
            "I've been doing so well."
          </p>

          <p>
            "I'll deal with it tomorrow."
          </p>

          <p>
            "It's not that big of a deal."
          </p>

          <p>
            "I know what I'm doing."
          </p>

          <p>
            And, of course:
            "Fuck it."
          </p>

          <p>
            These thoughts don't necessarily
            feel like excuses in the moment.
            They feel like reasonable
            explanations for why you
            should do what you want.
          </p>

          <p>
            Notice when your thinking shifts
            from weighing a decision
            to defending one you've
            already made emotionally.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ask yourself:
            Are you actually considering
            both options, or are you
            building a case for
            the one you prefer?
          </p>

        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 03 / YOUR INTERRUPTION PLAN -->
  <section class="relapse-plan-section relapse-plan-yellow">
    <div class="relapse-plan-container">

      <p class="relapse-plan-label">
        03 / YOUR INTERRUPTION PLAN
      </p>

      <h2>
        PUT SOMETHING<br>
        IN BETWEEN.
      </h2>

      <div class="relapse-plan-grid">

        <!-- PAUSE -->
        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>YOUR PAUSE</h3>

          <p>
            When you notice an impulse,
            stop the action before you
            start debating it.
            Put down your phone,
            leave the shopping app,
            or step away from
            whatever you're considering.
          </p>

          <p>
            Give yourself at least
            ten minutes before making
            a decision. For purchases,
            major commitments, or
            emotionally charged messages,
            consider waiting until
            the following day.
          </p>

          <p>
            During the pause, ask:
            What do you want?
            What are you expecting?
            What happens afterward?
            What would change if
            you waited?
          </p>

          <p>
            If you suspect mania,
            a short pause alone
            isn't enough. Delay
            consequential decisions,
            contact your psych, and involve a
            trusted person.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>
              Urgency is a feeling.
              It isn't a deadline.
            </strong>
          </p>

        </div>


        <!-- BARRIERS -->
        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>YOUR BARRIERS</h3>

          <p>
            Don't rely entirely
            on talking yourself
            out of drinking
            when the opportunity
            is already in
            front of you.
          </p>

          <p>
            Avoid liquor stores
            and drinking environments.
            Don't browse alcohol
            delivery services
            or make plans that
            put alcohol within
            easy reach.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you're tempted
            to buy something
            impulsively, close
            the shopping app,
            remove saved payment
            information, or ask
            someone you trust
            to help you delay
            the purchase.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you're tempted
            to send an angry
            or emotional message,
            write it in a
            private note instead
            of the conversation.
            Don't press send
            while your judgment
            feels compromised.
          </p>

          <p>
            If drinking has
            entered your plans,
            don't drive to
            obtain alcohol.
            Choose a safe
            place to stay
            and contact support.
          </p>

          <p>
            Make impulsive
            decisions harder
            to act on before
            you need those
            barriers.
          </p>

        </div>


        <!-- ALTERNATIVE ACTION -->
        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>YOUR ALTERNATIVE ACTION</h3>

          <p>
            You don't necessarily
            need to sit still
            and wait for the
            impulse to disappear.
            Give yourself another
            action to take.
          </p>

          <p>
            Get something to eat,
            make tea, listen to
            music, or change
            your surroundings.
            Spend time with Banjo
            or work on a small
            creative project.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want stimulation,
            choose something interesting
            that doesn't put your
            sobriety, money, or
            relationships at risk.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you're angry,
            take a break from
            the conversation.
            If you're overwhelmed,
            reduce the number
            of decisions you
            need to make. If you're overstimulated,
            move to a quiet space.
          </p>

          <p>
            Play the tape forward.
            Include the immediate
            reward, tomorrow morning,
            and what happens if
            the behavior becomes
            a pattern.
          </p>

          <p>
            You don't have to
            eliminate the desire.
            You need to choose
            what you do with it.
          </p>

        </div>


        <!-- CHECK-IN -->
        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>YOUR CHECK-IN PERSON</h3>

          <p>
            When you find yourself
            building a convincing
            case for drinking,
            involve someone
            before you act.
          </p>

          <p>
            Contact a trusted friend.
            Choose someone who will
            help you question
            the decision rather than
            encourage it.
          </p>

          <p>
            You can say:
            "I'm having a strong
            impulse to drink,
            and I'm starting
            to talk myself
            into it. Can you
            help me slow down?"
          </p>

          <p>
            If you're experiencing
            signs of mania, tell
            someone specifically
            what you've noticed:
            changes in sleep,
            increased energy,
            impulsive decisions,
            or unusual confidence.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your first person
            doesn't answer,
            contact another
            trusted person or someone from Harmony.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>
              You don't need
              to wait until
              you've made
              the decision
              to ask for help.
            </strong>
          </p>

        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 04 / IF THE URGE KEEPS BUILDING -->
  <section class="relapse-plan-section relapse-plan-purple">
    <div class="relapse-plan-container">

      <p class="relapse-plan-label">
        04 / IF THE URGE KEEPS BUILDING
      </p>

      <h2>
        WHAT'S YOUR<br>
        NEXT MOVE?
      </h2>

      <div class="relapse-plan-copy">

        <p>
          If the impulse doesn't settle
          after ten minutes, don't
          keep negotiating with it.
          Increase the amount of
          support and distance
          between yourself
          and the opportunity.
        </p>

        <p>
          Leave situations where
          alcohol is available.
          Avoid making purchases,
          sending messages,
          or committing to
          major plans while
          you feel unusually
          driven to act.
        </p>

        <p>
          Contact someone in
          your recovery circle
          and tell them directly
          that you're considering
          drinking. If necessary,
          attend a meeting
          or arrange to spend time
          with someone who
          supports your sobriety.
        </p>

        <p>
          If impulsivity comes
          with significantly reduced
          sleep, racing thoughts,
          elevated or irritable
          mood, or behavior that
          is becoming difficult
          to control, contact
          your psych promptly.
          Mania can impair
          judgment even when
          your decisions feel
          completely reasonable.
        </p>

       

        <p>
          If you've already acted
          impulsively, stop the
          sequence where you can.
          One bad decision doesn't
          require another.
          Tell the truth about
          what happened, address
          the consequences, and
          strengthen the safeguards
          that were missing.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>
            You can want something
            intensely and still
            decide not to do it.
            The impulse doesn't
            get the final vote.
          </strong>
        </p>

      </div>
<!-- BACK TO RELAPSE PLANS -->
